config CRC
	bool "crc: CRC-32 and CRC-16/IBM-3740"
	default y
	select STATUS
	help
	  CRC-32/ISO-HDLC and CRC-16/IBM-3740, computed incrementally over
	  caller memory. Freestanding: needs no runtime symbols, except
	  Zephyr's own CRC functions under CRC_ZEPHYR.

choice CRC_IMPLEMENTATION
	prompt "CRC implementation"
	default CRC_TABLE_4BIT
	depends on CRC
	help
	  What computes the CRCs: Elemental's own tables, or the compiler's
	  or the platform's code. Results are identical with every option,
	  and nothing a caller sees changes.

config CRC_TABLE_4BIT
	bool "16-entry tables (smaller)"
	help
	  Two table lookups per byte; 96 bytes of tables for both CRCs. The
	  right choice unless CRC throughput is measured to matter.

config CRC_TABLE_8BIT
	bool "256-entry tables (faster)"
	help
	  One table lookup per byte; 1.5 KiB of tables for both CRCs. For a
	  high-rate link on a target with flash to spare. Pigweed measured
	  the 256-entry CRC-32 at about 1.4 times the speed of the 16-entry
	  one on a Cortex-M4 (pw_checksum documentation).

config CRC_GCC_BUILTIN
	bool "GCC's CRC builtins"
	depends on COMPILER_GCC_CRC_BUILTINS
	help
	  __builtin_rev_crc32_data8 and __builtin_crc16_data8, from GCC 15.
	  For a target where GCC lowers them to a CRC instruction, which then
	  needs no source change. On x86-64, GCC 16 compiles them to a 1.5 KB
	  table, even with -mpclmul, so there they only cost size.

config CRC_ZEPHYR
	bool "Zephyr's CRC functions"
	depends on PLATFORM_ZEPHYR_CRC
	help
	  crc32_ieee_update() and crc16_itu_t() from <zephyr/sys/crc.h>, for a
	  Zephyr application that already links them, so the code is not
	  there twice. Needs CONFIG_CRC=y in the application, and is not
	  available with CONFIG_CRC_HW_HANDLER, whose driver calls can fail.

endchoice
